// shaper_csa_cr4rc_phase.v -- the CSA + CR-4RC shaper with a PHASE per hit (F07).
//
// GENERATED by gerar_fase.py (research vault, phase F07 of the Simulador_Pulsos_FPGA line)
// from shaper_csa_cr4rc.v. Identical to it except the pipelined datapath (PIPE_IN = 1): the four
// FIR taps and the input gains R1, R2 of the 51 ns and 41.5 ns sections are read, hit by hit,
// from MEM_PH (shaper_csa_cr4rc_phase.mif, 64 phases, phase k <-> phi = (k-32)*25/64 ns, phi > 0
// = the hit arrives later), and the FIR is in transposed form so every hit keeps its own phase.
// R0 (500 us) and the feedback coefficients are unchanged. Phase 32 gives the output of
// shaper_csa_cr4rc bit for bit. PIPE_IN = 0 (the paper build) has no phase. Do not edit by
// hand: rerun the generator after regenerating shaper_csa_cr4rc.v.
//
// COEF_SRC (2026-10-21, the configuration bus): "table" reads the 64 phases (the jitter), a RAM
// initialized from MEM_PH and writable through wr_*; "one" reads a single set of coefficients
// (the fixed phase, no table), the phase 32 + PHASE0 at power-up, writable through the same port.
//
// ---------------- the base file, as generated, follows ----------------
`timescale 1ns/100ps

// CSA + CR-4RC shaper: the generic "paper simulator" pulse.
//
// GENERATED by emitir_verilog.py (research vault, phase F03/F37 of the
// Simulador_Pulsos_FPGA line). Do not edit coefficients by hand: rerun the
// generator instead.
//
// The characteristic pulse is the EXACT readout chain of the group's papers
// (Electronics 14:493 signal generator): bi-exponential input (1/5 ns), CSA
// with a 51 ns feedback pole, and an unbuffered CR-4RC ladder with a 500 us
// CR and four 5 ns RC stages. Pulse: peak at 60.1 ns (on sample 2, sampling
// phase 10.10 ns), FWHM 114 ns, zero DC gain in continuous time, -2.5e-4
// tail out to ~0.5 ms.
//
// Structure: a 4-tap FIR head absorbs the 5 fast poles (their z-poles die
// within 2 samples; this includes the near-equal pair whose +-3.06e4 partial
// -fraction residues would cancel catastrophically in raw parallel fixed
// point) + 3 first-order direct-form IIR sections (tau = 500 us, 51 ns,
// 41.5 ns). Every arithmetic shift rounds to nearest (adds half an LSB
// first). Shape error of the integer model vs the exact kernel: 1e-7 of the
// peak (impulse 4095); 2.6e-6 for a small impulse (190).
//
// NOTE the kernel's SAMPLE SUM is -0.02466, not zero: the continuous pulse
// has exactly zero DC gain, but it is fast (5 ns edges), so summing samples
// at 25 ns aliases the 40 MHz harmonics (Poisson summation). The steady
// baseline is therefore -0.02466 * mean(input): that is the baseline-shift
// physics of the sampled chain that the papers describe, not a fixed-point
// artifact, and it is NOT removed here.
//
// Latency: 5 cycles with PIPE_IN = 1 (the default). Since 2026-10-03 the input
// was registered (latency 2); since 2026-10-14 everything outside the three
// section loops is pipelined too (R_k*x, the FIR products and sum, the final
// sum and rounding), the same values 3 cycles later: on the Cyclone V the
// simulator with this shaper goes 53.7 -> 79.2 MHz (worst corner), and the
// limit is now the section loop itself (Q_k*y_k in DSP blocks plus one add;
// an exact CSD shift-add tree of Q_k was slower, 76.8 MHz, and larger).
// PIPE_IN = 0 keeps the original single-cycle datapath, latency 1 (the paper
// build, macro USE_SHAPER_NO_PIPE). The shape is the same, only delayed.
//
// WARNING: the constants below bake in G_OUT_LOG = 10 (output scale 2**10,
// the HITS convention shared by the other shapers). The parameter exists for
// interface compatibility only.
module shaper_csa_cr4rc_phase
#(
	parameter BITS_IN = 34,
	parameter G_OUT_LOG = 10,
	parameter PIPE_IN = 1,
	parameter MEM_PH = "shaper_csa_cr4rc_phase.mif",
